Silicon carbide (SiC) solid-state transformers are advanced electrical power conversion systems that use semiconductor switching technologies and high-frequency magnetic isolation to transform and regulate electrical energy.
Unlike conventional transformers that primarily rely on electromagnetic induction at grid frequency, solid-state transformers incorporate actively controlled power electronics to provide additional capabilities, including voltage regulation, bidirectional power flow, and integration with AC and DC electrical distribution architectures.
The market is developing as data center operators, electrical equipment manufacturers, and technology providers seek compact, efficient, and flexible power conversion solutions capable of supporting increasingly demanding computing infrastructure.
Artificial intelligence computing, cloud services, and high-performance computing are creating substantial electricity requirements, encouraging investment in innovative power distribution technologies.
